From c00d113ad911d28e81f7140ea21fa3175f8d71b3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jernej Skrabec <jernej.skrabec@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 5 Sep 2026 09:02:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 207/211] drm/imagination: Force a hard reset when the GPU will
 not idle

A hard reset is what the driver reaches for once the firmware has
stopped answering, which after a real lockup usually means some unit is
wedged and the idle polls in pvr_fw_stop() time out. Treating that as
fatal marks the device lost with the core still powered and running, so
whatever it was doing keeps going into memory user space is about to
free. Two SoC hangs on the Cubie A7A followed exactly this sequence.

The power-off that comes next asserts the bus reset and gates the
clocks, which resets the core no matter what state it is in. Warn and
carry on with that instead of giving up.

Signed-off-by: Jernej Skrabec <jernej.skrabec@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/pvr_power.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/pvr_power.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/pvr_power.c
index e7a8a89a0fd..3610325417d 100644
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/pvr_power.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/pvr_power.c
@@ -550,6 +550,17 @@ pvr_power_reset(struct pvr_device *pvr_dev, bool hard_reset)
 		}
 
 		err = pvr_power_fw_disable(pvr_dev, hard_reset, false);
+		if (err && hard_reset) {
+			/*
+			 * A locked-up core never reports idle. Powering it off
+			 * below resets the whole thing, so carry on.
+			 */
+			drm_warn(from_pvr_device(pvr_dev),
+				 "GPU did not go idle for reset (%d), forcing it off\n",
+				 err);
+			err = 0;
+		}
+
 		if (!err) {
 			if (hard_reset) {
 				WRITE_ONCE(pvr_dev->fw_dev.initialised, false);
-- 
